<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Akuapem</h1><p class="st-meta">1733 CE – 1874 CE (approx.) · capital Akropong</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Akan and Guan state on the Akuapem ridge north of Accra, formed after the fall of Akwamu and later a centre of Basel Mission Christianity and Twi literacy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Akuapem ridge was home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guan</a>-speaking towns that had been subject to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/akwamu">Akwamu</a>. In 1733, after <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/akyem-abuakwa">Akyem Abuakwa</a> and its allies broke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akwamu">Akwamu</a> power, the ridge towns accepted an Akyem leader, Safori, as their ruler. He became the first Okuapehene, with his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akropong">Akropong</a>, and the new state joined Guan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> communities under one paramount chief.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Akuapem followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> political forms, with a paramount chief, divisional chiefs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odwira-festival">Odwira festival</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guan</a> towns kept their own traditions. Farmers on the ridge later led the expansion of cocoa growing in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-basel">Basel</a> Mission settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akropong">Akropong</a> in 1835, and missionaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottlieb-christaller">Johann Christaller</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akuapem-twi">Akuapem Twi</a> the basis of a written Twi language and Bible.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Akuapem came under British protection and was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony</a> in 1874. The Okuapehene still reigns at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akropong">Akropong</a>, and the town remains an important educational and church centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1733 CE – 1874 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.19%;width:2.81%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1733 CE – 1874 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Akropong</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Safori</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Akan traditional religion, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akuapem Twi, Guan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Akuapem State, Akwapim</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=akuapem" aria-label="Akuapem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4678 1954 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Akuapem lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4998 2210L4995 2226"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4995 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4998 2210h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4998 2210h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Akropong. <a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=akuapem">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
